<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The entitlements a NOTARISED build needs, and every one of them is here because of something
  this application actually carries rather than as a precaution.

  Notarisation requires the hardened runtime, and the hardened runtime's defaults are written for
  an application that is one signed binary. Jaroku's bundle is not: it ships a Node runtime as a
  Tauri sidecar and, under `~/.jaroku`, a standalone CPython that uv extracts and a tree of wheels
  containing compiled extension modules — pydantic-core, orjson, tiktoken, zstandard. Each
  entitlement below is what one of those needs.

  WITHOUT allow-jit AND allow-unsigned-executable-memory, V8 cannot allocate the pages it compiles
  JavaScript into and the Node sidecar dies at startup. That is not a Jaroku decision; it is what
  a JavaScript engine is.

  WITHOUT disable-library-validation, the Python interpreter cannot load an extension module
  signed by a different team — which is every wheel on PyPI, because they are signed by nobody.
  This is the entitlement to be uncomfortable about, and it is unavoidable for any application
  that runs a Python it did not compile. It is scoped by the fact that the interpreter only ever
  loads what `runtime/uv.lock` pins.

  WITHOUT allow-dyld-environment-variables, uv cannot set the interpreter's own library paths when
  it builds the environment.

  There is deliberately NO App Sandbox entitlement here. Jaroku runs code the user asked it to
  generate, writes to a directory of its own choosing and spawns processes; a sandboxed build
  would need a temporary exception for each of those, which is a longer list making a weaker
  claim. This is a developer tool distributed outside the Mac App Store, and the honest posture is
  hardened-and-notarised rather than sandboxed-with-holes.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
